Title: WHAT THE WATER KEEPS
Credit: Reference adaptation for testing
Author: (test kit - opening and final sequences)
Source: Based on the novella by M. L. Osorio
Draft date: 24 September 2026
Notes: Scenes 9-35 are outlined in the answer key, not written here.

.INT. MINISTRY DRAWING OFFICE - CITY - DAY #1#

A Ministry survey sheet, flat on a drafting table. Contour lines. Parcel numbers. In the lower corner, a single ruled line of blue ink and the words PROJECTED WATER LEVEL 412 M.

A woman's finger traces the blue line the length of the valley.

INES (V.O.)
My father said a map is a promise.

The printed valley becomes the real one: the same shape, suddenly green.

.EXT. BRIDGE AT VELL BAIXO - DAY #2#

APRIL 1957.

A country bus pulls away in its own dust. Left behind on the bridge: a theodolite in a wooden case, a levelling staff, two tripods, a case of drawing instruments, and one small suitcase.

INES MARO (29) stands among them like luggage at a station. She looks up the valley. Green. Much greener than paper.

Across the road, the door of the POST OFFICE opens. ODILE FARRE (60s, grey cardigan, arms folded) comes out and looks at the tripods.

ODILE
You'll be the surveyor.

INES
Ines Maro. From the Ministry.

ODILE
Odile Farre. Postmistress. I also keep the telephone, the register of deaths, and the key to the church. So if you want anything in this valley that isn't a sheep, you'll be wanting me.

Odile looks at the theodolite. Then at Ines.

ODILE (CONT'D)
They said a surveyor. They didn't say a girl.

INES
I'm twenty-nine.

ODILE
That's what I said.

Odile picks up the levelling staff and shoulders it.

INES
I can carry that.

ODILE
Not in Vell you can't.

.EXT. VILLAGE LANE - CONTINUOUS #3#

Odile climbs, staff on her shoulder. Ines follows with the theodolite case hugged to her chest.

Ahead, a TRUCK is being loaded outside a farmhouse: chairs, a mattress, a birdcage. A FAMILY works in silence.

ODILE
Gates close the end of September. Forty-three families have signed. Thirty-one are arguing about the money. Sixteen haven't answered the letters.

INES
And the rest?

ODILE
One. He burned his.

A TELEPHONE rings inside the post office behind them. Odile doesn't turn.

ODILE (CONT'D)
Tomas Aurelio. Keeps bees at the narrows. He'll not sign. His grandmother's grandmother is under the church floor. You'll want his land and he'll not let you, and I'm telling you now so you'll not say no one told you.

.INT. INN - TOP ROOM - NIGHT #4#

A narrow room. One window up the valley.

The suitcase is unpacked: three dresses, a hairbrush. Done.

The instruments take the rest of the night. Ines cleans the theodolite part by part on a cloth, the way someone cleans a thing they love.

Last from the suitcase: a folded sheet, soft with age. She opens it.

A hand-drawn floor plan of a small apartment, scale 1:20, every chair drawn in. A child's bed exactly the length of the kitchen table. In the hallway, a tiny inked X, labelled in a man's hand: WHERE INES LOST THE TOOTH.

She pins it above the bed.

.EXT. RIVER VELL - THE NARROWS - DAY #5#

The valley pinches. A flat FERRY hangs on a rope across the river.

On the far bank, rows of white HIVES stand in a slope of wild thyme like a small, orderly town. Among them, a veiled figure moves slowly.

Ines waits on the near bank with her tripod.

A long time.

The figure comes down to the water and lifts the veil. TOMAS AURELIO (35). The face of a man who has been told many things by officials and believed none of them.

TOMAS
The Ministry.

INES
I need the height of your land above the river. For the compensation.

TOMAS
I know what for.

INES
Two days. I won't disturb the hives.

TOMAS
You'll disturb them by being here. They know when something's being counted.

He tugs the ferry rope once, idly. The boat knocks the bank.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
And when you've measured it?

INES
I draw it. The Ministry sets the compensation from the drawing.

TOMAS
And then they drown it.

INES
Yes.

This surprises him. He looks at her properly.

TOMAS
At least you don't lie. The last one lied. He said the lake would be beautiful.

INES
It probably will be.

TOMAS
So is a grave, if you keep it tidy.

He goes back up among the hives.

.EXT. THYME HILL - DAWN #6#

Ines drives survey stakes into the far bank's slope. Each has a strip of red cloth.

She takes a small pot of BLUE PAINT from her bag. One stake, the one level with the Ministry line, gets a band of blue.

The blue stake stands halfway up the slope, just below the cottage door.

.EXT. THE NARROWS - FERRY - NEXT DAWN #7#

Ines arrives at the ferry with her tripod.

On the deck of the ferry: her stakes, pulled up, stacked in a neat pile. On top of them, a jar of HONEY.

She looks up the slope. No one.

.MONTAGE - STAKES AND HONEY #8#

- Ines drives the stakes in again. The blue one last.

- Dawn: the stakes on the ferry deck. Two jars of honey.

- Ines drives them in again, harder.

- Dawn: the stakes on the deck. Three jars.

.EXT. THE NARROWS - DAY #9#

No stakes today. Ines sits on the bank with three jars of honey in a row beside her, and waits.

Tomas comes down. Stops at a distance.

INES
I can make the survey without your land. I'll estimate it from this side. Estimates run low. You'll be paid for land ten metres lower than it is.

TOMAS
Then they'll have paid me less to drown more.

INES
Yes.

TOMAS
You're a strange sort of Ministry.

INES
I'm a surveyor. The Ministry's in the city.

He sits on the bank, a little way off. Looks at the water.

TOMAS
Two days.
(beat)
And you'll write down the names.

INES
What names?

TOMAS
Of the fields. Everything here has a name. Your map has numbers.

He stands.

TOMAS (CONT'D)
You want my land, you take the names with it.

He goes. Ines looks at her field book. On the page: PARCEL 212. She writes beneath it, small: NAME?

