.INT. INN - TOP ROOM - DAWN #36#

SEPTEMBER.

Out of the window: the lower valley is a lake. Grey, flat, full of the tops of trees. The bridge is gone. The mill is gone.

Up the far slope, a thin grey line of chimney smoke rises from Tomas's cottage. The blue stake below his door is under water.

Ines is dressed, packed, sitting on the bed. Her suitcase at her feet.

Outside, a TRUCK HORN. Twice.

She doesn't move.

The horn again. Then the truck's engine, leaving.

She watches the smoke.

.INT. INN - FRONT ROOM - LATER #37#

Chairs stacked on tables. The register of deaths sits on the bar beside Odile's handbag. Odile has moved in.

Odile joins Ines at the window. They watch the smoke together.

ODILE
He's moved his bees. Up to the high pasture, on the Dog. Took him three nights. Carried every hive himself.

INES
Then he's leaving.

ODILE
He's moved his bees. He hasn't moved himself.

She lets that sit.

ODILE (CONT'D)
He went up to the church yesterday with blankets.
(beat)
His grandmother's grandmother is under that floor. I told you that the day you came.

INES
I know.

ODILE
You'll not say no one told you.

.INT. MINISTRY ARCHIVE - CITY - NIGHT #38#

[[IMPLIED - reported in C11; dramatised here, withholding what she writes]]

Rows of flat steel drawers. One lamp.

Ines alone at a slanted table with a sheet of linen, a ruling pen, a bottle of black ink. Her lips move silently as she works. Her body hides the sheet. What she is writing stays hers.

.EXT. THE LAKE - DUSK #39#

The inn's rowing boat, low in still water. Ines rows alone over the drowned valley.

Beneath the hull, just visible through the water: a stone wall. The top of a gate. A drowned field.

She steers without looking round. She has measured every metre of it.

The CHURCH stands up out of the lake on its rise like a ship. The door is under. Only the tower and the roof ridge are dry.

She ties the boat to a window grille of the tower and climbs in.

.INT. CHURCH TOWER - TOP - NIGHT #40#

Lamplight. The BELL, enormous, green-black, the crack in its lip.

Tomas sits against the wall beside it. A blanket. A lamp. A jar of honey. He is not surprised.

TOMAS
Go back. It's rising a metre a day.

INES
I know. I measured it.

TOMAS
Then you know how long I have.

INES
Six days to this floor. Eight to the bell.

She sits on the top step. Where she sat in April.

INES (CONT'D)
I brought you something.

She slides an oilcloth tube from her coat. Unrolls it on the stone between them. Weighs the corners down with the lamp, the honey jar, her two hands.

Linen. The engineer's stamp: VALLEY OF VELL - SISTERS RESERVOIR - ARCHIVE COPY.

And beside every parcel number, in black ink, in small careful capitals: THE WIDOW'S PIECE. ANSELMO'S EYE. LITTLE LISBON. THYME HILL. THE DOG.

Tomas stares at it.

INES (CONT'D)
They took the names off. In the drawing office. So I put them back. At night. I told the archivist I'd found an error in the contours.
(touching the linen)
This is the copy they keep. Forever. Nobody will ever look at it again. But it will be there.

TOMAS
Why?

INES
A map is a promise. That the place will still be there when you come back.
(beat)
Even if it isn't. My father told me that.

He looks up at the bell.

TOMAS
And the bell?

INES
Your grandmother didn't know who it belonged to. Maybe it doesn't belong to anyone. Maybe it just rings.

A long moment.

TOMAS
You went in for the box. In April. I thought you were a fool.

INES
I was.

TOMAS
So am I.

He rolls up the map.

.EXT. THE LAKE - NIGHT #41#

The boat moves away from the tower. The lamp in the bow. The water so still the church stands in it twice, once upward, once down.

Tomas rows. Ines faces back toward the tower.

Silence. Oars. Nothing else.

Then: one long, flat, cracked note from the tower. It rolls up the valley and comes back.

Not a leaf moves. No wind at all.

Tomas stops rowing. They sit in the drifting boat and listen until there is nothing left to listen to.

.EXT. LAKESHORE - NEW WATERLINE - DAY #42#

The last Ministry TRUCK waits, engine running. Ines's suitcase and instruments are loaded.

Tomas stands with her at the edge of the new lake. Above them on the slope: the hives, moved to the Dog. Beside them, a new stone hut.

He gives her a jar of honey. She gives him the working map: pencil, coffee rings, every name.

TOMAS
Come back.

INES
There's nothing to come back to.

TOMAS
There's the Dog. And the thyme.
(beat)
And the names.

She gets into the truck. It pulls away along the new shore.

In the water below the road, the top of a blue-painted stake, just breaking the surface.

.INT. INES'S OFFICE - CITY - DAY #43#

SUPER: JUNE.

A drafting table under a window. A MESSENGER sets down a small parcel. No letter.

Ines opens it: a jar of honey. She opens the jar. Smells it. Thyme.

On the shelf behind her: four more empty jars, lined up.

.EXT. OLD QUARTER - CITY - WINTER NIGHT #44#

YEARS LATER.

Ines, older, walks home through a crowded street.

A church bell rings once from somewhere she can't see: one long flat note, a little cracked. It goes up the street and comes back.

She stops. People flow around her. She stands until there is nothing left to listen to.

.INT. INES'S APARTMENT - NIGHT #45#

A clean sheet of paper. A ruling pen. Beside the drafting table, pinned to the wall, a child's floor plan at 1:20.

Ines draws the first contour of a valley.

FADE OUT.
